  ],
  "text": "Skeletons from the first farming villages are shorter and sicker than the hunter gatherers before them. Farming is supposed to be the upgrade. More food, easier life. It did deliver more food. That part is not in question. But more food out of fewer plants. Three crops instead of a hundred wild foods. A narrower diet means missing nutrients, and it shows up in the bones and the teeth. And living packed together with livestock invented the infectious diseases we still live with. So why did anybody keep doing it? Because a field feeds more people per acre than a forest does, even done badly. Populations grew. Villages became cities. Specialists appeared. Writing followed. Farming was a great deal for the species and a bad deal for almost everyone in it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
